.. thumbnail-parent-div-open

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Hund and Mulliken&#x27;s molecular-orbital theory builds one-electron orbitals spread over the whole molecule as a linear combination of atomic orbitals, \psi_\pm=c_A\chi_A\pm c_B\chi_B. H2PlusVariational does this for H2+ with one Gaussian orbital per proton: diagonalizing the 2x2 problem gives the in-phase bonding orbital (equal coefficients, energy below the isolated-atom level) and the out-of-phase antibonding orbital (opposite coefficients, energy above it). Following both orbital energies from short bond lengths out to separated atoms gives Mulliken&#x27;s correlation diagram for the simplest molecule.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/quantum/hartree_fock/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_lcao_molecular_orbitals_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/hartree_fock/plot_01_lcao_molecular_orbitals`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Hund-Mulliken LCAO molecular orbitals of H2+: bonding and antibonding</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/hartree_fock/plot_01_lcao_molecular_orbitals`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Walter Heitler and Fritz London&#x27;s 1927 quantum-mechanical treatment of H2 -- the paper usually credited with founding quantum chemistry as a field -- showed for the first time why two hydrogen atoms bind: the symmetric (in-phase) combination of their two atomic orbitals builds up electron density in the region between the nuclei, lowering the energy below that of the separated atoms, while the antisymmetric (out-of-phase) combination depletes density there and raises the energy instead. Their original calculation used two-electron integrals this package does not implement (chemistrykit.quantum.utils.basis_sets stops at the one-electron integrals H2PlusVariational needs for H2+), but the same qualitative physics -- constructive vs. destructive interference of atomic orbitals along the bond axis -- is exactly what H2+&#x27;s own bonding and antibonding molecular orbitals show, using the genuine LCAO coefficients solve computes, not an assumed equal-weight combination.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/quantum/hartree_fock/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_02_bonding_antibonding_density_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/hartree_fock/plot_02_bonding_antibonding_density`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Heitler-London's insight: bonding vs. antibonding electron density</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/hartree_fock/plot_02_bonding_antibonding_density`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Born and Oppenheimer separated the fast electrons from the slow nuclei: first solve the electronic problem with the nuclei clamped at a fixed separation R, then let the nuclei move on the resulting potential energy curve U(R)=E_{el}(R)+e^2/4\pi\varepsilon_0R. Here each point of the curve is a separate clamped-nuclei calculation with H2PlusVariational (``bond_length`` is a fixed input, never a dynamical variable). The curvature at the minimum then gives a nuclear vibrational frequency, and because the electronic curve does not depend on nuclear mass, H2+ and D2+ share the same curve but have different zero-point energies.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/quantum/hartree_fock/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_03_born_oppenheimer_potential_curve_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/hartree_fock/plot_03_born_oppenheimer_potential_curve`

.. raw:: html

